{"type":"data","nodes":[null,{"type":"data","data":[{"mission":1,"hero":492},{"id":2,"agency":3,"agency_full":4,"sector":5,"dest":6,"color":7,"year":8,"status":9,"departure_date":10,"arrival_date":11,"transit_days":12,"vehicle":13,"payload":14,"delta_v":15,"data_quality":16,"credit":17,"links":18,"flight_data_quality":46,"fleet_refs":47,"flight":53,"name":480,"type":481,"first":482,"description":483,"events":484,"vehicle_stage":478},"dawn","NASA","National Aeronautics and Space Administration / Jet Propulsion Laboratory","gov","CERES","#0B3D91",2007,"FLOWN","2007-09-27","2015-03-06",2717,"Delta II 7925H","Ion-propelled orbiter; framing camera, VIR, GRaND, etc.","~11 km/s (approx)","partial","© NASA / JPL-Caltech — U.S. Government work, public domain. Vesta rendezvous phase precedes Ceres; catalogue shelf is Ceres per ADR-028.",[19,23,27,30,34,37,40,43],{"l":20,"u":21,"t":22},"Dawn (spacecraft) — Wikipedia","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dawn_(spacecraft)","intro",{"l":24,"u":25,"t":26},"Dawn mission archive (NASA)","https://science.nasa.gov/mission/dawn/","core",{"l":28,"u":29,"t":26},"Dawn at JPL","https://www.jpl.nasa.gov/missions/dawn/",{"l":31,"u":32,"t":33},"NSSDCA catalog — Dawn (2007-043A)","https://nssdc.gsfc.nasa.gov/nmc/spacecraft/display.action?id=2007-043A","deep",{"l":35,"u":36,"t":33},"Vesta interior structure — Russell et al. 2012, Science 336","https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.1219381",{"l":38,"u":39,"t":33},"Ceres bright spots = sodium carbonate — De Sanctis et al. 2016, Nature 536","https://www.nature.com/articles/nature18290",{"l":41,"u":42,"t":33},"Ceres subsurface brine reservoir — Castillo-Rogez et al. 2020, Nature Astronomy 4","https://www.nature.com/articles/s41550-020-1019-1",{"l":44,"u":45,"t":33},"Ion propulsion subsystem — Brophy 2011, Space Science Reviews 163","https://doi.org/10.1007/s11214-011-9848-y","sparse",[48,51],{"id":49,"role":50},"delta-ii","launcher",{"id":2,"role":52},"spacecraft",{"events":54,"interplanetary_profile":83,"launch":477},[55,60,65,70,75,78],{"met_days":56,"type":57,"label":58,"description":59},0,"launch","Launch — Delta II 7925H","Cape Canaveral, 2007-09-27. First spacecraft designed to orbit two destinations — only possible because of NSTAR ion engines (xenon propellant) providing 10× the specific impulse of chemical thrust. The low-thrust trajectory takes years to build the velocity changes needed but uses 10× less propellant.",{"met_days":61,"type":62,"label":63,"description":64},510,"flyby","Mars — gravity assist","Closest approach 542 km on 2009-02-18. The Mars assist added ~2.6 km/s — enough to reach asteroid Vesta after another 2 years of ion-thrust spiralling. Without the assist, Dawn would have needed twice the propellant load or twice the cruise time.",{"met_days":66,"type":67,"label":68,"description":69},1388,"arrival","Vesta orbit insertion","Captured into Vesta orbit on 2011-07-16 via continuous ion-thrust gravitational capture — no chemical orbit-insertion burn was needed because the ion thrust handled the gradual approach. Spent 14 months in Vesta orbit, mapping the 530-km-diameter differentiated proto-planet and confirming the south-polar Rheasilvia impact basin as the source of HED meteorites that reach Earth.",{"met_days":71,"type":72,"label":73,"description":74},1805,"tli_or_tmi","Vesta departure — ion-thrust spiral to Ceres","Departed Vesta on 2012-09-05. Ion engines spiralled outward over 2.5 years toward Ceres — the first interplanetary transfer ever executed under continuous low thrust between two bodies. The trajectory used ~112 kg of xenon.",{"met_days":12,"type":67,"label":76,"description":77},"Ceres approach","Approach phase begins ~2015-02. Dawn observed Ceres's bright spots in Occator crater for weeks before formal orbital capture, revealing them as briny salt deposits — evidence of a subsurface aqueous reservoir on the dwarf planet.",{"met_days":79,"type":80,"label":81,"description":82},2717.53,"edl_or_oi","Ceres orbit insertion","Captured into Ceres orbit on 2015-03-06 — the first spacecraft to orbit a dwarf planet, and the first to orbit two extraterrestrial bodies. Spiralled down to a 35-km low-altitude mapping orbit by 2018. Ran out of hydrazine for attitude control on 2018-10-31; the spacecraft remains in stable Ceres orbit, a long-term protected planetary monument under planetary-protection rules.",{"source_tier":84,"reference_frame":85,"departure_body":86,"arrival_body":87,"transfer_type":88,"waypoints_helio_au":89},"tier_1_5_hybrid","heliocentric_ecliptic_J2000","earth","ceres","type1_lambert",[90,93,97,101,105,109,113,117,121,125,129,133,137,141,145,149,153,157,161,164,168,172,176,180,184,188,192,196,200,204,208,212,216,220,224,228,232,236,240,244,248,252,256,260,264,268,272,276,280,284,288,292,296,300,304,308,312,316,320,324,328,332,336,340,343,347,351,355,359,363,367,371,375,379,383,387,391,395,399,403,407,411,415,419,423,427,431,435,439,443,447,451,455,459,463,467,469,471,473,475],[56,91,56,92],0.995467,0.095107,[94,95,56,96],28.941,0.884619,0.132962,[98,99,56,100],57.881,0.784464,0.164316,[102,103,56,104],86.822,0.694768,0.18937,[106,107,56,108],115.763,0.620175,0.206249,[110,111,56,112],144.704,0.552908,0.219575,[114,115,56,116],173.645,0.491994,0.229871,[118,119,56,120],202.585,0.439957,0.23629,[122,123,56,124],231.526,0.392982,0.240715,[126,127,56,128],260.467,0.349942,0.24363,[130,131,56,132],289.408,0.312247,0.244644,[134,135,56,136],318.348,0.278138,0.244506,[138,139,56,140],347.289,0.246557,0.243586,[142,143,56,144],376.23,0.218293,0.241706,[146,147,56,148],405.171,0.192643,0.239143,[150,151,56,152],434.111,0.168679,0.236159,[154,155,56,156],463.052,0.146835,0.232677,[158,159,56,160],491.993,0.126949,0.22878,[61,162,56,163],0.11488,0.22629,[165,166,56,167],549.628,0.090898,0.22025,[169,170,56,171],578.39,0.075072,0.21559,[173,174,56,175],607.151,0.060078,0.210794,[177,178,56,179],635.913,0.046015,0.205849,[181,182,56,183],664.675,0.033134,0.200729,[185,186,56,187],693.437,